{"task_id": "Mbpp/259", "solution": "from typing import Any, Sequence, Tuple\ndef maximize_elements(a: Sequence[Sequence[Any]], b: Sequence[Sequence[Any]]) -> Tuple[Tuple[Any, ...], ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes element-wise maxima across two nested sequences.\n    The result is returned as a tuple of tuples.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_rows = []\n    for row_a, row_b in zip(a, b):\n        current_row_maxima = []\n        for x, y in zip(row_a, row_b):\n            current_row_maxima.append(max(x, y))\n        result_rows.append(tuple(current_row_maxima))\n    return tuple(result_rows)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/260", "solution": "def newman_prime(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the nth Newman\u2013Shanks\u2013Williams number S(n) using matrix exponentiation.\n    The sequence is defined by S(0)=1, S(1)=1, S(n)=2*S(n-1) + S(n-2) for n >= 2.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Validate input\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a non-negative integer\")\n\n    # Handle base cases directly as per the sequence definition.\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n    if n == 2:\n        # S(2) = 2*S(1) + S(0) = 2*1 + 1 = 3\n        return 3\n\n    # Define matrix multiplication for 2x2 matrices as an inline helper.\n    def _matrix_mul(m1: tuple, m2: tuple) -> tuple:\n        a1, b1, c1, d1 = m1\n        a2, b2, c2, d2 = m2\n        return (a1 * a2 + b1 * c2, a1 * b2 + b1 * d2,\n                c1 * a2 + d1 * c2, c1 * b2 + d1 * d2)\n\n    # The base matrix for the NSW recurrence is ((2,1),(1,0)).\n    # To find S(n), we need to compute M^(n-1).\n    # If M^(n-1) = ((a,b),(c,d)), then S(n) = a*S(1) + b*S(0).\n    # Since S(1)=1 and S(0)=1, this simplifies to S(n) = a + b.\n    BASE_MATRIX = (2, 1, 1, 0)\n\n    # Compute BASE_MATRIX^(n-1) using binary exponentiation.\n    exponent = n - 1\n\n    result_matrix = (1, 0, 0, 1)  # Identity matrix for multiplication\n    current_base = BASE_MATRIX\n\n    while exponent > 0:\n        if exponent & 1:  # If the current bit is 1, multiply result by current_base\n            result_matrix = _matrix_mul(result_matrix, current_base)\n        current_base = _matrix_mul(current_base, current_base)  # Square the base\n        exponent >>= 1  # Move to the next bit\n\n    # Extract 'a' and 'b' from the resulting matrix M^(n-1).\n    a, b, _, _ = result_matrix\n\n    # Calculate S(n) = a + b.\n    return a + b"}
